                <pb facs="0439" n="343" sameAs="frus:frus1899/pg343"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hay to Mr. Tower.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1899-05-26"><hi rendition="#italic">May 26,
                        1899</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1460.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to previous correspondence
                    relative to the course of the American minister at La Paz in protecting the
                    interests of British subjects in Bolivia, and particularly with regard to your
                    note of the 17th ultimo on the subject, I have the honor to inform you that the
                    Department has received a dispatch from Mr. Bridgman, our minister at La Paz,
                    reporting that at present there is no need of protection for British subjects in
                    Bolivia as everything is quiet there.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Hay.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
